---
passage_id: dostoevsky-ivan-rebellion
source_text: "And if the sufferings of children go to swell the sum of sufferings which was necessary to pay for truth, then I protest that the truth is not worth such a price. I would rather remain with my unavenged suffering and unsatisfied indignation, even if I were wrong. Too high a price is asked for harmony; it is beyond our means to pay so much to enter on it. And so I hasten to give back my entrance ticket."
---

# Propositions
[P1]: Suppose eternal cosmic harmony requires the uncompensated torture and suffering of innocent children as a necessary means. {type: counterfactual_assumption, credence: 0.9500, speaker: "Ivan Karamazov", span: 7..104, quote: "the sufferings of children go to swell the sum of sufferings which was necessary to pay for truth"}
[E1]: No teleological or utilitarian future good can morally justify using the unredeemed suffering of an innocent child as an instrument (deontological side-constraint). {type: enthymeme, credence: 0.8800, speaker: "Ivan Karamazov (Implicit)"}
[P2]: Ultimate truth and eschatological harmony are morally unworthy of being purchased at the price of innocent children suffering. {type: explicit_premise, credence: 0.0000, speaker: "Ivan Karamazov", span: 126..161, quote: "the truth is not worth such a price"}
[P3]: The moral cost of participating in such a cosmic harmony exceeds what conscience can legitimately pay. {type: explicit_premise, credence: 0.0000, speaker: "Ivan Karamazov", span: 264..356, quote: "Too high a price is asked for harmony; it is beyond our means to pay so much to enter on it."}
[E2]: If accepting a cosmic order requires consenting to an immoral price, a moral agent must refuse participation in that order even if the order is metaphysically real. {type: enthymeme, credence: 0.7500, speaker: "Ivan Karamazov (Implicit)"}
[C]: Therefore, I morally refuse to accept the cosmic harmony and return my entrance ticket. {type: thesis, credence: 0.0000, speaker: "Ivan Karamazov", span: 357..405, quote: "And so I hasten to give back my entrance ticket."}

# Support Hyperedges
<H1>: [P1] & [E1] +> [P2] {weight: 0.9500, scheme: consequence}
<H2>: [P2] +> [P3] {weight: 0.9200, scheme: modus_ponens}
<H3>: [P3] & [E2] +> [C] {weight: 0.9500, scheme: practical_reasoning}
